# 1. What is Docker and why is it used in DevOps?
Docker is a platform for building, packaging, distributing, and running applications in
containers. It allows developers to package an application with its required dependencies and
filesystem components into a Docker image.
That image can then be used to run containers across compatible environments, reducing
differences between development, testing, and production. Docker is widely used in DevOps
because it supports consistent deployments, application isolation, and automated CI/CD
workflows.
For example, instead of manually installing Python and its libraries on every server, a team
can package a Python application into a Docker image and run it wherever a compatible
container runtime is available.

